artifact.self_contained_problem:
Observations of distant galaxies and quasars exhibit systematic redshifts z of their spectral features that increase with apparent distance. In the standard cosmological framework these redshifts are interpreted as a consequence of the metric expansion of the universe (with the wavelength-independent scaling λ_obs = (1+z) λ_emit and an associated time-dilation factor (1+z) for source light curves). Several alternative, non-expansion mechanisms have been proposed to account for the observed redshifts, including:

(i) gravitational redshift from compact or massive configurations along the line of sight,
(ii) "tired light" hypotheses in which photons lose energy en route through some dissipative interaction with the intergalactic medium,
(iii) variable-mass theories in which atomic emission frequencies evolve with cosmic time,
(iv) chronometric cosmology in which redshift arises from a reparametrization of time on a static background,
(v) multiple-scattering models in which redshift is induced by repeated low-angle scattering events.

Determine whether any such non-cosmological redshift mechanism can simultaneously satisfy all of the following observational constraints:
 (a) it must reproduce the observed redshift–distance relation for galaxies and quasars;
 (b) it must not produce angular blurring of distant sources beyond the levels actually observed in high-resolution imaging;
 (c) it must be achromatic to the precision with which spectra of distant sources are observed to redshift uniformly across wavelength;
 (d) it must be consistent with the (1+z) time-dilation observed in light curves and spectra of Type Ia supernovae;
 (e) it must be consistent with the precise blackbody spectrum of the cosmic microwave background and with limits on spectral distortions;
 (f) it must not violate constraints on the density, temperature, and opacity of the intergalactic medium derived from quasar absorption-line systems and other probes.

Formally: does there exist a physically consistent non-expansion redshift mechanism meeting (a)–(f), or do these observational constraints together imply that the observed redshifts require a genuinely expanding universe?

